1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,781
Alder had 13 readmissions among
its 30 patients. Is that a lot?

2
00:00:04,821 --> 00:00:10,287
In Episode 3 we got two answers.
Against everyone pooled together,

3
00:00:10,327 --> 00:00:16,350
1.4 times what was expected. Against
everyone but Alder itself, 1.8.

4
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:21,293
So we asked for two things: a risk rule
learned from patients who share a clinic,

5
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:24,119
and a yardstick no single clinic can bend.

6
00:00:24,159 --> 00:00:28,157
The risk rule arrived with the
dial. Today, the yardstick.

7
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:32,403
Here is the idea. Keep Alder's
30 patients exactly as they are,

8
00:00:32,443 --> 00:00:35,748
and send them, on paper, to the middle clinic.

9
00:00:35,788 --> 00:00:39,359
Would they have more than 13
readmissions there, or fewer?

10
00:00:40,933 --> 00:00:43,154
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

11
00:00:43,194 --> 00:00:45,102
Today: swap the curve.

12
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:52,088
First, the yardstick. Line up the five
sliders, and take the one in the middle.

13
00:00:52,128 --> 00:00:56,334
It is Cedar's, at minus 1.52.
And as we saw last time,

14
00:00:56,374 --> 00:01:00,734
it stays put wherever Elm's
slider happens to stop.

15
00:01:00,867 --> 00:01:04,304
Draw that clinic's curve in
grey, and call it the reference.

16
00:01:04,344 --> 00:01:09,796
At the reference, a lower-risk patient
has an 18% chance of readmission.

17
00:01:09,836 --> 00:01:12,246
A higher-risk patient, 62%.

18
00:01:12,367 --> 00:01:16,615
Notice what kind of thing the
reference is. Not an average patient.

19
00:01:16,655 --> 00:01:20,717
A clinic: one clinic's level,
applied to whoever walks in.

20
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:27,251
Here are Alder's patients
on Alder's own curve:

21
00:01:27,291 --> 00:01:32,327
24 lower-risk patients at
34%, and 6 higher-risk at 80%.

22
00:01:32,367 --> 00:01:34,922
Add up those chances, and you get 13.

23
00:01:34,962 --> 00:01:39,309
Exactly what Alder observed,
because the fit balanced it.

24
00:01:39,433 --> 00:01:47,990
Now swap the curve. The same 30 patients,
on the grey one: 24 at 18%, and 6 at 62%.

25
00:01:48,030 --> 00:01:50,748
Add those up: 8.02.

26
00:01:50,867 --> 00:01:55,643
So at the reference, these same patients
would expect about 8 readmissions.

27
00:01:55,683 --> 00:01:57,504
At Alder, there were 13.

28
00:01:57,544 --> 00:01:59,400
13 over 8.02 is 1.62.

29
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:05,000
Alder had 62% more readmissions
than its own patients would be

30
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:09,094
expected to have at the reference clinic.

31
00:02:09,233 --> 00:02:13,615
That is the whole method. Keep the
patients. Swap the curve. Count.

32
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:21,429
Do the same for every clinic.
Alder, 1.62. Birch, 0.88.

33
00:02:21,469 --> 00:02:26,765
Cedar, exactly 1. Dune, 1.46. And Elm, zero.

34
00:02:26,805 --> 00:02:32,361
Cedar comes out at exactly 1, because it
is the reference, measured against itself.

35
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:38,583
So Alder's answer is neither
1.4 nor 1.8. It is 1.62.

36
00:02:38,623 --> 00:02:43,074
And this yardstick is hard to bend.
Push any one slider as far as you like,

37
00:02:43,114 --> 00:02:47,357
and the middle value moves no
further than the next clinic along.

38
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:52,454
One thing has changed since Episode
3. Against the pooled standard,

39
00:02:52,494 --> 00:02:56,032
the expected counts added
up to the 42 observed.

40
00:02:56,072 --> 00:02:59,855
Against the middle clinic,
they add up to 38.8.

41
00:02:59,895 --> 00:03:03,829
The clinics no longer break even
as a group, and they need not.

42
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:08,280
Ratios are often reported as rates.

43
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:13,189
Multiply each one by the
overall readmission rate, 41%.

44
00:03:13,229 --> 00:03:20,939
Alder becomes 66%. Birch, 36.
Cedar, 41. Dune, 59. Elm, zero.

45
00:03:21,067 --> 00:03:26,534
Read these with care. No patient at
Alder had a 66% chance of readmission.

46
00:03:26,574 --> 00:03:30,868
It is the same ratio, moved
to a more familiar scale.

47
00:03:32,467 --> 00:03:38,678
One more connection. Move Alder's slider,
and its ratio traces a single rising curve.

48
00:03:38,718 --> 00:03:45,041
With the slider at the reference, the
ratio is 1. At Alder's own gamma, 1.62.

49
00:03:45,167 --> 00:03:50,744
So every statement about a gamma
becomes a statement about a ratio.

50
00:03:50,784 --> 00:03:56,865
Episode 9's interval for Alder's gamma
becomes an interval for its ratio:

51
00:03:56,905 --> 00:03:58,765
from 1.02 to 2.31.

52
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:04,838
Two cautions. First, a ratio of 1
means like the reference clinic.

53
00:04:04,878 --> 00:04:06,742
It does not mean good.

54
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:09,656
Second, always say which reference.

55
00:04:09,696 --> 00:04:15,476
Last time we tried a size-weighted
average instead of the middle clinic.

56
00:04:15,516 --> 00:04:19,100
Against that, Alder's 1.62 becomes 1.87.

57
00:04:19,233 --> 00:04:22,080
In our crowd of 200 providers,

58
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:28,366
the ratios run from 0.44 to 1.99,
with half on each side of 1.

59
00:04:28,406 --> 00:04:33,258
Which of those are real differences, and
which are luck? That question is coming.

60
00:04:34,833 --> 00:04:36,329
Let's gather it up.

61
00:04:36,369 --> 00:04:38,953
The reference is the middle clinic's curve.

62
00:04:38,993 --> 00:04:44,308
Keep a clinic's patients, swap in that
curve, and count: observed over expected.

63
00:04:44,348 --> 00:04:46,938
And name the reference with every ratio.

64
00:04:47,067 --> 00:04:50,309
We kept the patients and
swapped the curve. Next time,

65
00:04:50,349 --> 00:04:54,286
the other way round: keep the
curve, and swap the patients.

66
00:04:54,433 --> 00:04:58,538
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
